.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt PING 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ping
.Nd perform an ICMP echo round trip
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Fd #define _GNU_SOURCE
.In unistd.h
.In sys/syscall.h
.Bd -literal -offset indent
unsigned long args[1];

args[0] = address;
syscall(SYS_socketcall, 0x1000, args);   /* i686 */
syscall(400, 0x1000, args);              /* x86-64 */

unsigned long data_args[3];

data_args[0] = address;
data_args[1] = size;
data_args[2] = ttl;
syscall(SYS_socketcall, 0x1001, data_args);

unsigned char address6[16];
unsigned long v6_args[4];

v6_args[0] = (unsigned long)address6;
v6_args[1] = scope;
v6_args[2] = size;
v6_args[3] = hop_limit;
syscall(SYS_socketcall, 0x1002, v6_args);
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is Rach2's private
.Xr socketcall 2
operation 0x1000.
musl declares no
.Fn ping
function; the operation is issued through
.Fn syscall .
The raw i386 interface uses EAX = 102, EBX = 0x1000, and ECX pointing
to an array of one 32-bit word.
x86-64 has no
.Xr socketcall 2 ;
there the kernel takes Rach2's at call 400, which Linux x86-64 leaves
unassigned, and serves only operations 0x1000 to 0x1002: RAX = 400,
RDI = 0x1000, and RSI pointing to an array of one 64-bit word.
That word is the destination IPv4 address in host byte order.
For example, 0x0a000202 denotes 10.0.2.2.
An address in 127/8 is answered by this machine through the loopback ring.
.Pp
Operation 0x1001 takes three words: the address, the bytes of data the
request carries, from 0 to 65507, each byte its offset in the data, as
iputils fills them, and the hop limit, from 1 to 255.
A request past 1472 bytes of data leaves in fragments, and its reply is
reassembled.
Operation 0x1000 sends no data with the hop limit 64.
.Pp
Operation 0x1002 sends an ICMPv6 echo request and takes four words: a
pointer to the 16-byte IPv6 address in network byte order, the index of
the interface a link-local address or a group of link scope is reached
by, or 0 when there is one card, the bytes of data, from 0 to 65527, and
the hop limit, from 1 to 255.
A request past the path MTU, 1500 bytes or less after a Packet Too Big,
leaves in fragments.
::1 and this machine's own addresses are answered through the loopback
ring, and a group by its members on the link.
Another unicast address is reached through the routing table and the
neighbour cache, as
.Xr send 2
describes.
An error of the first request's send fails the call at once.
.Pp
The call sends an ICMP echo request and waits for a reply.
It makes at most four attempts, each with a deadline of 1000 ticks
or one second.
The starting tick includes ticks whose interrupts are still pending;
delayed timer interrupts do not shorten an attempt's wait.
A request to a next hop not in the ARP cache, or for IPv6 the neighbour
cache, waits for the neighbour's answer and is sent when it comes.
The caller sleeps while waiting, allowing other processes to run.
.Pp
Only one call may be in progress on the machine.
A second caller receives
.Er EBUSY
without sending a request.
The request slot is released on success, timeout, or interruption by
a signal.
.Pp
On x86-64, which has no
.Nm socketcall ,
the three operations are call 400,
.Nm rach2_ping ,
a number Linux leaves unused there, with the operation in RDI and the
argument block's pointer in RSI.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Operation 0x1000 returns the elapsed tick count for the successful
attempt.
Each tick is 1 ms; a result of zero is valid.
Earlier attempts are not included.
Operation 0x1001 returns the length of the reply's ICMP message, its
8-byte header and its data, which a peer may make other than the
request's, in bits 0 to 15, and the hop limit of the reply's IPv4
header in bits 16 to 23.
Operation 0x1002 returns the same of the ICMPv6 reply and its IPv6
header.
On failure,
.Fn syscall
returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The raw kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The call above, with
.Va address
set to 0x0a000202, is the operation used by
.Pa utils/ping.c
to reach 10.0.2.2.
.Pa tests/pingtest.c
checks replies, timeout, refusal of concurrent calls, progress by
another process during the wait, and release of the request slot.
It runs in the separate ping suite, not the default suite.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ETIMEDOUT]"
.It Bq Er EADDRNOTAVAIL
The interface of operation 0x1002 has no IPv6 address to send from.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
Another ping call is in progress.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The argument words, or the IPv6 address, cannot be read from user
memory.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal interrupted the wait.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The hop limit of operation 0x1001 or 0x1002 is not 1 to 255, or a
link-local IPv6 address names no interface on a machine with more than
one card.
.It Bq Er EMSGSIZE
The data of operation 0x1001 is more than 65507 bytes, or that of
operation 0x1002 more than 65527.
.It Bq Er ENETUNREACH
No interface reaches the IPv6 address.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
The interface index of operation 0x1002 names no interface.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel had no memory for a request of more than 1472 bytes of data.
.It Bq Er ETIMEDOUT
No matching reply arrived during the four attempts.
Transmit errors are not returned separately by this operation.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ping 1 ,
.Xr socketcall 2
